  "prompt_text": "The figure summarizes a proposed pathway for breast cancer progression from normal mammary epithelium to metastasis, highlighting how female hormones may influence multiple stages through signals in the tissue microenvironment.\n\nAcross the bottom, the tissue changes from a normal duct, to an initiated cell within otherwise normal epithelium, then to carcinoma in situ, invasive carcinoma, and finally a cluster of metastatic cells. The drawings emphasize the structural shift from an organized epithelial layer around a hollow lumen to a crowded, abnormal mass that eventually breaks through the duct boundary and spreads.\n\nAt the top, progesterone and oestrogens are shown as major upstream regulators. Arrows connect these hormones to several mediators, including amphiregulin, RANKL, cyclin D1, and ID4, with WNT marked more tentatively (“WNT?”), suggesting some uncertainty. These factors appear to act at different transition points, especially around the move from initiated lesions to carcinoma in situ and then to invasive disease. Large curved arrows from the hormones toward later stages imply broader, stage-spanning effects.\n\nOverall, the image conveys that hormone-driven signaling in the breast microenvironment may promote both early transformation and later invasion and metastasis.",
